/-! ## The Gram certificate behind completeness

The enumeration above starts from an orthogonal quadruple.  Completeness runs
in the other direction: `D16` supplies an arbitrary four-root basis.  The
following finite certificate classifies the Gram matrix of that basis and the
possible pairing columns of roots in its span.  All data stay integral.  If
`G` is the Gram matrix, `H = adj(G)`, and `s = det(G)`, a pairing column `d`
has coordinate numerator `u = Hd`; it represents a root precisely when

    u^T G u = 8 s^2.

There are only `5^6` symmetric Gram matrices with diagonal `8` and
off-diagonal entries in `{0, +-4, +-8}`, and `5^4` pairing columns.  A
non-positive Gram type carries a checked nonzero vector `z` with
`z^T G z <= 0`.  Every remaining type has a checked two-sided scaled inverse,
at most twelve pairing columns modulo sign, and, at the maximum twelve, an
orthogonal quadruple whose Parseval identities cover every admissible column.
The computations below are the oracle; the lemmas after them prove why the
oracle applies to an arbitrary `D16` witness.
-/

/-- The five possible pairings, decoded from one base-five digit. -/
@[expose] public def pairVal (n : Nat) : Int := 4 * (((n % 5 : Nat) : Int) - 2)

/-- A four-entry pairing column, encoded in four base-five digits. -/
@[expose] public def pairVec (n : Nat) : Vec 4 Int :=
  fun i => pairVal (n / 5 ^ i.val)

/-- The off-diagonal slot of a symmetric `4 x 4` matrix. -/
@[expose] public def gramSlot (i j : Fin 4) : Nat :=
  let a := Nat.min i.val j.val
  let b := Nat.max i.val j.val
  if a = 0 then b - 1 else if a = 1 then b + 1 else 5

/-- A symmetric Gram type: diagonal `8`, six base-five off-diagonal digits. -/
@[expose] public def gramType (n : Nat) : Mat 4 4 Int :=
  fun i j => if i = j then 8 else pairVal (n / 5 ^ gramSlot i j)

/-- The integral bilinear form attached to a matrix. -/
@[expose] public def form4 (M : Mat 4 4 Int) (x y : Vec 4 Int) : Int :=
  Vec.sumInt (fun i => x i * Vec.sumInt (fun j => M i j * y j))

/-- The 393 positive cases carry their determinant and adjugate as one packed
certificate.  The determinant occupies thirteen bits; the sixteen signed
adjugate entries occupy eleven bits each after adding `512`. -/
